This study examines the semileptonic decays of the $B$ meson, specifically $B 	o \ell  ar{
u} + X$, where $X$ represents any hadronic final state. We demonstrate that, within the Standard Model, the ratio of decay rates for $B 	o \ell  ar{
u} + X$ and $B 	o \ell'  ar{
u} + X$ is precisely equal to the mass ratio $m_\ell / m_\ell'$ for any charged lepton $\ell$ and $\ell'$. This result holds true regardless of the specific composition of the hadronic system $X$, the decay energy transfer, or the $B$ meson's polarization. The validity of this relationship is confirmed through both theoretical calculations and numerical simulations. Furthermore, we show that new physics scenarios can be identified by analyzing the dependence of this ratio on the hadronic invariant mass, denoted as $s_+$.

